Lords of the Fallen is clearly inspired  by Dark Souls but it's important not to  get bogged down by too many comparisons  between the souls games and this one  Lords of the Fallen is distinct enough  to earn its own spotlight and perhaps to  earn your love as well this is an  entertaining and Atmospheric adventure  and one that deserves a sequel that  further flushes out its fantasy world  world might be too generous a word  actually you spend most of your time in  corridors and moody combat arenas not  gazing on two massive landscapes you  roam from a third-person perspective  swinging an axe or sword dodging or  blocking incoming attacks and  occasionally spewing out some magic an  energy meter depletes when you roll an  attack forcing you to manage your  defenses so you aren't vulnerable to  damage different melee weapons require  different approaches but Lords of the  Fallen gives each of them an authentic  sense of weight it takes time to swing  that humongous great sword you carry and  improper timing leaves you open to  counter-attacks  you also have spells to cast in a  gauntlet that shoots out magic  projectiles among other attacks casting  the limited spells you can choose from  when leveling up and switching between  those spells is a matter of pressing or  holding a button there's no need to  switch from a dagger to a wand if you  want to produce a speeding figure that  runs right through your enemies for a  good amount of damage there are no bows  and arrows and Lords of the Fallen it's  all swords and sorcery  you  the game insists on trying to tell story  but the early hours are pretty  incoherent and antihero Harkin doesn't  have much interesting to say anyway  instead it's better to let the frozen  walkways and imposing monastery speak  for themselves  this place seeks to murder you consider  the titles of the bosses you fight beast  champion Annihilator they have one  purpose to kill to call Lords of the  Fallen up for man's Dark Souls sells it  too short for one Lords of the Fallen  strikes a different kind of tone its  Moody and oppressive but rarely  terrifying it is a power fantasy not a  death simulator and the chunky ornate  art style reflects the difference Lords  of the Fallen has a few challenges but  it's rare for you to feel frail or  afraid the game simply isn't hard enough  to make your blood boil that's at least  true in the main world the visits you  make to a shadowy and sinister  Otherworld are a little more frightening  traversing this dark dimension is like  exploring a foggy desert at midnight you  can barely see further than the tip of  your blade your steps into the beyond  first lead you just simple Knights and  ghouls which require only that you put  the clumsy targeting system to good use  soon however you could encounter our  rolling fire demon that puts up a bit  more fight you reach this realm by  entering portals that only unlock and  you've killed some unknown beasts hoping  to find some special treasure like a new  hammer or a rune for upgrading your  equipment you die but not frequently  enough for Lords of the Fallen to get  grindy or frustrating when you perish  you leave behind your ghost and revive  at whichever Ruby crystal you last saved  at your ghost contains all the  experience you've accumulated since the  prior death but it doesn't remain  forever so it's in your best interests  to go retrieve it at least early on  every fallen enemy will have respawned  after your death but you'll at least be  armed with the knowledge of  what lies ahead of you death is not a  huge setback though experience can be  regained easily and in the last several  hours you earn too little experience  from killing enemies to mind the loss  the bosses may have ominous titles but  if you have souls experience you can off  most of them on the first attempt the  challenge ramps up nicely during the  lead-up to the final showdown however in  a few bosses particularly the final one  demand a bit of extra thinking the  greatest challenge Lords of the Fallen  provides isn't a welcome one it's easy  to lose yourself in the crypts and  corridors unsure of where the game means  for you to go every region cleverly  connects with others but some areas are  so same ish and some entrance is so  subtle that the game can become aimless  the annoyances are few however you could  call Lords of the fall in a souls game  for the meek at 20 hours the first time  through it's not as long as the games  that inspired it nor is it as special as  hard or as mysterious but Lords of the  Fallen has a consistency of tone a  powerful spirit weighty combat and a  beautiful world to explore and it's one  of the biggest pleasant surprises 2014  you
 

